# Windows batch files must use CRLF line endings. cmd.exe's label scanner
# (goto/call :label) fails to resolve labels in an LF-only .cmd/.bat, so pin
# these to CRLF on every checkout regardless of the user's core.autocrlf.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# CPE-1042: the sample-data baseline must be byte-exact and reproducible on every
# platform. Treat all of samples/ as binary so git never applies EOL conversion
# (a CRLF-munged PDF/MP3/etc. would no longer match the generated baseline).
samples/** -text

# CPE-1880: the harness scripts under scripts/ are imported by vitest through Vite's
# transform pipeline, and a .mjs checked out with CRLF makes that transform throw
# `SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token` with a location that points at a comment
# on line 2 — i.e. no usable diagnostic at all. It reproduces ONLY on a checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true (Windows dev boxes); the Linux CI runner checks out LF and stays
# green, so CI cannot catch it and the next person loses an hour. Pin them to LF on
# every checkout regardless of the user's autocrlf setting, the same way the .cmd/.bat
# rule above pins the opposite direction for cmd.exe.
scripts/*.mjs text eol=lf
